---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const CALENDLY = 'https://calendly.com/russell-decisive/30min';

const title = 'Finance for manufacturers | Decisive';
const description =
  'A sale price is a multiple times earnings. We grow the earnings your products actually make and make them believable to a buyer, in 14 days.';

const traps = [
  {
    name: 'Anchoring',
    body: 'Pricing anchored to input costs that moved years ago is the most common trapped margin on a factory floor. A buyer re-prices it against you the moment they see the standard costs are stale.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Mental Accounting',
    body: 'A clean aggregate P&L hides which SKUs and lines carry the business and which quietly bleed. Diligence runs the product-line math you never did. Better you find the cross-subsidy first.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Loss Aversion',
    body: 'The largest customer is often the least profitable, and the concentration a buyer discounts hardest. The account you are most afraid to reprice is usually the one capping your margin.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <header class="hero">
    <div class="hero-inner">
      <span class="eyebrow">For manufacturers and consumer products companies</span>
      <h1>
        <span class="h1-line">You can't sell a maker on numbers</span>
        <span class="h1-line h1-line-accent">that hide which products pay.</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="hero-subhead">
        A sale price is a multiple times earnings. <span class="dollar">We work both numbers:</span> we grow the earnings your products actually make, and we make them believable to a buyer. Most makers can't see either from the P&amp;L they have.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-cta-row">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={CALENDLY}>Book a fit call</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/how-it-works">See the 14-day Scan</a>
      </div>
      <p class="hero-microcopy">30 minutes with Russ. No pitch. You leave knowing what a buyer would re-price, and what you could recover.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="container-narrow">
        <span class="section-eyebrow">What a buyer re-prices in a product business</span>
        <h2>Revenue is easy to see. Which product makes money is not.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          A buyer of a manufacturer tests input-cost drift, phantom discounts, standard versus actual cost, product-line and SKU profitability, and customer concentration. Every soft answer is a discount, and every one is also margin you can recover before you go to market.
        </p>
        <div class="category-pull">
          Accurate and ugly beats polished and untrustworthy. Buyers know the difference.
        </div>
        <p>
          The mess isn't negligence. Growth covered it, and the people producing the numbers were never paid to look. Everyone who touches your books closes the month. Nobody is making your company worth believing, and that is the job we do. The day a sale, a PE approach, or a succession comes into view, every soft number converts from a chronic annoyance into priced risk, and the clock starts.
        </p>
        <div class="delay-callout">
          The trailing-twelve clock doesn't start until the numbers are true. Every month you wait moves the sale date a month.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="traps">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-eyebrow">Where the value is trapped</div>
      <h2>Three traps that hide inside a clean P&amp;L.</h2>
      <p class="lede" style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.88);">
        The Six Trap Diagnostic&trade; reads your numbers the way a buyer will. Three of the six do the most damage in a product business. The patterns are structural, not failures of the owner.
      </p>
      <div class="trap-grid">
        {traps.map((t, i) => (
          <div class="trap-card">
            <div class="trap-num">Trap {String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</div>
            <div class="trap-name">{t.name}</div>
            <p>{t.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="traps-footer">
        We name the stack, price each one two ways, what a buyer discounts and what you can recover, and turn the read into a plan. We don't audit the past. We make the company worth believing.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="alt">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="container-narrow">
        <span class="section-eyebrow">Proof</span>
        <h2>Two product companies. The gap was never the market. It was the numbers.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="worked">
        <span class="label">D2C food manufacturer, $25M revenue</span>
        <h4>The gap was never the market. It was the numbers.</h4>
        <p>
          The banker's estimate was $25M to $35M, on books the owner didn't trust. Mispriced inputs, phantom discounts, and revenue never brought over. Pricing was anchored to costs that had moved years earlier, and the aggregate P&amp;L hid which products carried the business. We rebuilt the numbers a buyer could believe and modeled the path forward.
        </p>
        <div class="calc">
          <div><span class="k">Banker's estimate</span><span class="v">$25M to $35M</span></div>
          <div><span class="k">Modeled path</span><span class="v accent">roughly $52M</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="worked" style="margin-top: 24px;">
        <span class="label">Retail food manufacturer, $15M revenue</span>
        <h4>Concentration a buyer discounts is margin you can recover before you go to market.</h4>
        <p>
          The largest customer was the least profitable. The account felt untouchable, so the math never got run, and full cost allocation showed it was funded by everyone else. We repriced and reset terms instead of protecting the logo.
        </p>
        <div class="calc">
          <div><span class="k">Least profitable account</span><span class="v">The largest one</span></div>
          <div><span class="k">Margins moved</span><span class="v accent">15% to 22%</span></div>
          <div><span class="k">Time to recover</span><span class="v">90 days</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="font-size: 14px; color: var(--muted); margin-top: 22px;">
        Anonymized by segment and revenue band until attribution is confirmed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="container-narrow">
        <span class="section-eyebrow">Tiers and pricing</span>
        <h2>Find. Install. Run.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Most owners start with the 14-day Sellable-Numbers Scan and move into the 90-Day Resolution. Pricing is banded by revenue, and the floor is $5M.
        </p>
        <p class="promise-line">
          The Scan promise: at least 3x the fee in owner-accepted value identified by Day 14, or you pay nothing.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-cta-row" style="margin-top: 8px;">
          <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/#tiers">See tiers and pricing</a>
          <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/guarantee">Read the guarantee</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="close-section">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="section-eyebrow">Book a fit call</span>
      <h2>Decide from the numbers. Prove it in the cash.</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        In 14 days you see your company the way a buyer will, in dollars, on both sides of the equation. You leave with the re-price read, the worth read, and the first three moves, whether or not we ever work together again. It starts with one 30-minute call.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-cta-row">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={CALENDLY}>Book a fit call</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/how-it-works">See the 14-day Scan</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .hero-microcopy { font-size: 15px; color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.7); margin: 10px 0 0; }
  .promise-line { font-family: Inter, sans-serif; font-weight: 600; color: var(--ink); margin-top: 8px; }
</style>
